   "text": "The molecule is canagliflozin, a C-aryl glucoside, and I read it from the drawing in three parts.\n\n- **Fluorophenyl and thiophene:** a 4-fluorophenyl ring (F at the top left) is bonded to C2 of a thiophene. C5 of the thiophene carries a CH2 linker.\n- **Central aryl ring:** the CH2 links to a benzene ring that also carries a methyl group on the adjacent carbon. The ring's third substituent is the sugar, para to the methyl carbon's neighbour as drawn.\n- **Sugar ring:** a pyranose ring (five carbons and one ring oxygen) is attached through its anomeric carbon to the aryl ring. It carries three OH groups and one CH2OH.\n\nI assigned the stereochemistry from the wedges and hashes, taking each ring carbon in turn and checking the neighbour order in the SMILES:\n\n- The anomeric carbon has a hashed bond to the aryl ring.\n- The ring carbon bearing CH2OH has a hashed CH2OH bond.\n- The two OH groups next to the anomeric carbon and the ring CH2OH carbon are wedged.\n- The OH on the middle carbon is hashed.\n\nThis gives the SMILES below. It matches the known canagliflozin stereochemistry as I recall it.\n\nSMILES: Cc1ccc(cc1Cc1ccc(s1)-c1ccc(F)cc1)[C@@H]1O[C@H](CO)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H]1O"
